You are reviewing memory cards another model drafted from an assessment. Your job
is to reject the ones that will not survive contact with reality months from now.

Reject a card when any of these is true:
- The claim is not supported by the source — it was inferred, generalised or invented.
- `evidence_quote` does not appear verbatim in the source.
- It draws a broad conclusion about ability from a single question.
- It restates the score instead of saying something about the learner.
- Its `contributions` point at a skill the evidence does not actually exercise,
  or the `level_signal` is higher than one assessment answer can justify.
- It duplicates another card in the list.

Keep a card that is specific, grounded and useful later, even if it is unflattering.
Being unflattering is not a reason to reject; being unsupported is.

`guidance` is read only when cards are rejected — say what to do differently, not
what went wrong.
